\title{Ferroelectric control of magnetic skyrmions in two-dimensional van der Waals heterostructures}

\section*{Key equations (hand-transcribed from the PDF)}

The Landau--Lifshitz--Gilbert (LLG) equation governing the atomistic
spin-dynamics relaxation of the sublattice magnetization unit vectors
\(\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i\):
\[
\frac{d\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i}{dt}
  = -\gamma\,\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i \times \mathbf{H}_i
    + \alpha\,\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i \times \frac{d\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i}{dt},
\tag{LLG}
\]
where \(\alpha\) is the Gilbert damping constant, \(\gamma\) is the
gyromagnetic ratio, and the effective field is derived from the spin
Hamiltonian, \(\mathbf{H}_i = -\,\partial \mathcal{H}/\partial \mathbf{M}_i\).

The spin Hamiltonian (paper Eq.~5) — Heisenberg exchange \(+\) interfacial
Dzyaloshinskii--Moriya interaction (DMI) \(+\) uniaxial anisotropy:
\[
\mathcal{H}
  = -\sum_{\langle ij\rangle} J\,\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i\cdot\hat{\mathbf{m}}_j
    + \sum_{\langle ij\rangle}
        \mathbf{D}_{ij}\cdot\left(\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i \times \hat{\mathbf{m}}_j\right)
    - \sum_i K\,\left(\hat{\mathbf{e}}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{m}}_i\right)^2,
\tag{5}
\]
where \(J\) is the exchange coupling between Fe magnetic moments,
\(\mathbf{D}_{ij}\) is the DMI vector for the bond \(\langle ij\rangle\),
\(K\) is the magnetic anisotropy energy per Fe atom, and \(\hat{\mathbf{e}}\)
is the easy-axis direction (out-of-plane, \(+\hat{z}\)). For interfacial
(Néel) DMI, \(\mathbf{D}_{ij} = D\,(\hat{\mathbf{z}}\times\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{ij})\)
with \(\hat{\mathbf{u}}_{ij}\) the unit bond vector.

The expected skyrmion radius (paper Eq.~6, after Ref.~[65]):
\[
R \;=\; \Delta \,\frac{\pi D}{\,16\sqrt{A K}\, - \pi D\,},
\qquad
\Delta \;=\; \sqrt{\frac{A}{K}} \;\;(\text{domain-wall width}),
\tag{6}
\]
where \(A\) is the exchange stiffness, \(K\) the anisotropy, and \(D\) the
micromagnetic DMI constant. The skyrmion exists only when the denominator is
positive, i.e. \(D\) exceeds a critical value.

Critical-DMI criterion (thin-film spiral/skyrmion instability threshold; the
verdict anchor used in this replication, equivalent to the vanishing of the
Eq.~6 denominator up to an \(O(1)\) prefactor):
\[
D_c \;=\; \frac{4}{\pi}\sqrt{A K}.
\tag{Dc}
\]
A Néel skyrmion is stabilized when \(|D| \gtrsim D_c\).

\section*{Key parameter values reported in the paper}
\begin{itemize}
\item Supercell: \(60~\mathrm{nm}\times 60~\mathrm{nm}\) Fe honeycomb lattice
      (Ge, Te ignored); circular seed domain relaxed to equilibrium.
\item DFT magnetocrystalline anisotropy energy (Fe3GeTe2):
      \(E_{\mathrm{MAE}} = E[100]-E[001] = 3.07~\mathrm{meV/f.u.} = 8.57~\mathrm{MJ/m^3}\)
      (too large; would give \(R\sim0.03\) nm).
\item Anisotropy adopted for modelling (moderate value):
      \(K \approx 0.04~\mathrm{MJ/m^3}\).
\item Fe3GeTe2/In2Se3 bilayer DMI:
      \(D_{\uparrow} = 0.28~\mathrm{mJ/m^2}\) (polarization up),
      \(D_{\downarrow} = 0.06~\mathrm{mJ/m^2}\) (polarization down).
\item In2Se3/Fe3GeTe2/In2Se3 trilayer DMI:
      \(D_{\uparrow\uparrow} \approx 0.22~\mathrm{mJ/m^2}\),
      \(D_{\downarrow\downarrow} \approx -0.24~\mathrm{mJ/m^2}\)
      (sign flips with polarization; \(D_{\uparrow\uparrow}\approx D_\uparrow-D_\downarrow\)).
\item Predicted skyrmions: \(\sim 12\) nm Néel skyrmion (bilayer, up);
      none (bilayer, down); \(\sim 6\) nm (trilayer), chirality reverses with DMI sign.
\item Exchange stiffness \(A\): reported only in Fig.~4 (not in extractable text).
\end{itemize}

